# Lets GitHub show a "Cite this repository" button and gives the project a
# stable, machine-readable citation. It is also what Zenodo reads when a release
# is archived to mint a DOI.
#
# The name matches paper/rl_trader.tex and the other CITATION.cff files in these
# projects. The site uses "Dan" as a display byline, which is a separate thing
# from a citation name.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "AlphaGlyph — a backtesting laboratory that measures its own credibility"
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Daniel
    family-names: Lichtenberger
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Danny-397/Alphaglyph"
url: "https://alphaglyph.org"
abstract: >-
  AlphaGlyph is a strategy backtester built around the tests that try to
  disprove a result rather than confirm it: a random-timing permutation test,
  the Deflated Sharpe Ratio, Fama-French factor attribution, a transaction-cost
  sensitivity sweep, combinatorially-purged cross-validation, and an
  append-only forward track record that grades predictions after the fact.
  Applied to its own 42-variant hyperparameter grid it reports a 72%
  probability of backtest overfitting, with a median of roughly half the
  in-sample Sharpe surviving out of sample and an inverted in-sample to
  out-of-sample degradation slope. An earlier run of the identical procedure on
  a different rolling window returned 63%, and that instability is reported as
  a finding: a single probability-of-overfitting figure quoted as a constant is
  the exact error the metric exists to catch.
keywords:
  - backtesting
  - probability of backtest overfitting
  - deflated sharpe ratio
  - combinatorially purged cross-validation
  - quantitative finance
  - reproducibility
license: MIT
date-released: "2026-10-20"
